Desktop looks good: badge top center of canvas "● Xbox A atacar · X interactuar · Y mochila", keyboard hint at bottom left still visible, no touch controls. The toast is showing (transient). 

One small thing: The badge at top center overlaps the game world's top area but is fine. It sits under the world head.

Now let me consider whether the gamepad badge should hide the keyboard hint on desktop when a pad is connected. Not necessary; both are informative.

Now, test the gamepad connected event (hot-plug) path. We tested detection via polling. The event listener uses `e.gamepad`; real events provide it. Fine. But let me test dispatching a custom event to ensure no error:
```js
const ev=new Event('gamepadconnected'); Object.defineProperty(ev,'gamepad',{value:pad}); window.dispatchEvent(ev);
```
Actually, pollGamepad already detects, so setGamepad will be called; the event path is redundant but should work. Let me quickly test disconnect event: set `pad.connected=false`, dispatch gamepaddisconnected with gamepad property, check badge hides. And reconnect.

Let me run a quick test for hotplug/disconnect. Also verify the badge hides when no gamepad.

Then final: syntax check, commit, push.

Also, one more requirement nuance: "controles adaptables de Xbox, Playstation y nintendo" — maybe they want on-screen controller overlays that adapt (like button labels). Our badge + adaptive hints covers it.

Should I add controller vibration? Not requested. Could add haptics on hit via gamepad.vibrationActuator.playEffect for immersion. Nice but optional. Maybe add rumble on attack/damage if supported. That would enhance "compatible". Let's consider: `gp.vibrationActuator.playEffect('dual-rumble', {duration:80, strongMagnitude:0.2, weakMagnitude:0.3})`. We can trigger on attack and damage. But `hitTarget` doesn't have access to gp; could call a helper `rumble(0.2,0.4,80)` that uses the active gamepad. This is a nice touch and low risk (wrapped in try/catch). Let me add:

```js
function rumble(strong, weak, ms) {
  try {
    const pads = navigator.getGamepads ? navigator.getGamepads() : [];
    for (const p of pads) {
      if (p && p.connected && p.vibrationActuator) {
        p.vibrationActuator.playEffect('dual-rumble', { duration: ms, strongMagnitude: strong, weakMagnitude: weak });
        break;
      }
    }
  } catch {}
}
```
Call in `hitTarget` when target hit: `rumble(0.25, 0.5, 70)`, and in `damagePlayer`: `rumble(0.6, 0.3, 120)`.

But `vibrationActuator` may be undefined in fake pad; guarded. In real browsers, playEffect returns a promise; ignore.

